    45	
    46	## Result
    47	
    48	On the contract text, **nothing Tessera runs at verification time reaches the verdict.** Tessera affects a
    49	verdict in two ways only, and C6 permits both:
    50	1. It wrote the bundle bytes before shipping (row 1).
    51	2. It makes the verifier release and the trust file that the checker installed (rows 3-5). Their authenticity is
    52	   the distribution assumption (A9:50-52). Identical output is promised only for identical declared inputs,
    53	   the configuration included (PROF:530-531; A9:267-270).
    54	
    55	So G8 holds as worded, if "the inputs above" includes the request and the compiled-in catalog (F1). G8 is a
    56	claim about the decision, not about who chose the release.
    57	
    58	## Findings
    59	
    60	- **F1 (CLARIFY).** C6 (A9:145) and G8 (VC:21, :32) name three inputs. The profile adds the request policy
    61	  (PROF:220) and a compiled-in catalog (PROF:100-101). PROF:154-155 says "Nothing is taken from outside the bundle
    62	  except the trust configuration", which omits both. Reading the catalog as part of "the published verifier"
    63	  covers it; the request is a checker input with one legal value. Proposed: name both in the contract.
    64	- **F2 (contract wording).** VC:62 says a different "pin set can select a different block". It does not say that
    65	  a later release can also turn an `UNVERIFIABLE` verdict into `VALID_STRICT` (A9:337-339). A relying party who
    66	  re-verifies under a newer release can see a different verdict.
    67	- **F3 (open, after exit).** The TEST interface takes the release key and catalog from the environment
    68	  (PROF:321-325). That is a verdict-relevant input outside C6's list. A10 requires production to reject TEST
    69	  authority and a rehearsal that shows it, before the first public verifier release (A10:644-657). Until then,
    70	  only TEST releases exist (A10:650-651).
    71	- **F4 (CLARIFY).** No-network and no-clock are stated as claims (A9:33; PROF:1224; A10:307-308) and for DNSSEC
    72	  only as a rule (PROF:864-866). The profile has no general rule "the verifier reads no input but these". The
    73	  20 Nov code inspection (A9:214) has no sentence to inspect against.
    74	- **F5 (open, frozen by 30 Oct).** The "published verifier" is not yet an object: the release format and
    75	  release-key custody are OPEN (PROF:1148-1158; A10:624-627). A10 freezes the format by 30 Oct (A10:639-641).
    76	- **F6 (open).** The dependency manifest "is a schema for a future object, not the object" (PROF:990-994);
    77	  Unicode tables, the ECMA-262 edition and SHA-1DC are not yet pinned (PROF:1219-1225). Input 6 is declared but
    78	  not yet fixed.
    79	- **F7 (tracker).** `formal/PROPERTIES.md:44` cites the standing probe's Q6 for the input list. That row lists
    80	  "trust configuration only (channel public keys, `k`, `δ`)" (`formal/spike/standing-probe/RESULTS-PROBE.md:41`).
    81	  This is stale: δ, ε and k moved to the catalog (PROF:144-145, :382-386). `docs/V1-EXIT-30OCT.md:104` cites a
    82	  different file (`formal/spike/tla-probes/P9/RESULTS-PROBE.md`). This page supersedes both as the V1 input list.
    83	- **F8 (schedule).** A4.1's second leg, the two-isolated-machine vector (`docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-4.md:67-70`),
    84	  is on neither A9.2 list (A9:205-216). The derivation places it at H1a (`docs/V1-DERIVATION-DRAFT.md:109`); the
    85	  contract records it as not built (VC:93, :100). P9 stays `open` until it runs.
