     1	# Using a Tessera V1 receipt: a guide for users
     2	
     3	> **OWNER DRAFT 2026-10-19, awaiting non-author review.** (Claude Opus 5.5, owner instance.) The E12 page of the
     4	> 30 Oct exit (A9.2, `docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md:208`; registered by
     5	> `docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-3.md:89-95`). It is based on `docs/V1-CONTRACT.md` (owner draft). It is written in
     6	> the plain style of `docs/plain/STE-STYLE.md`. It adds no rule. If it differs from A9, A10, the V1 profile or the
     7	> contract, they apply and this page is wrong. Markers such as [R4] point to the references at the end. The 4 Dec
     8	> relying-party test will test this page; that test has not occurred [R27].
     9	
    10	## What Tessera can establish, and what it does not establish
    11	
    12	This list is copied without change from the 2026-07-28 ruling [R1]. Its words "package", "payload" and "issuer"
    13	are the ruling's words.
    14	
    15	> Tessera can establish:
    16	> "This valid package reconstructs the same framed bytes recorded
    17	> at issuance."
    18	>
    19	> Tessera does not establish:
    20	> "The payload is true."
    21	> "The issuer understood it."
    22	> "The relying party interpreted it correctly."
    23	> "This was the package the relying party expected."
    24	> "The external thing named by the payload is authentic."
    25	
    26	## What a VALID V1 receipt tells you
    27	
    28	A receipt is the set of files that the service gives back for one content. The service is the Tessera V1
    29	program that makes receipts. A verifier is the program that checks a receipt. If the verifier says VALID, these
    30	statements are true, under the conditions in "Conditions that V1 does not check" below [R2]:
    31	
    32	1. The receipt fixes these exact content bytes. A change of one bit makes the check fail [R3].
    33	2. Someone gave these bytes to the Tessera V1 service on some occasion [R3].
    34	3. Both service keys signed them. A missing or changed signature makes the check fail [R3].
    35	4. Two authority records named the two keys for `wamason.com`. One record is in DNS. The other is in a signed git
    36	   commit that Bitcoin recorded at least 6 blocks before the receipt [R4].
    37	5. The bytes and both signatures existed before a specific Bitcoin block. The verifier reports that block's
    38	   chain time as T [R4].
    39	6. The issue time that the service wrote is not before the start of the keys' window. T is not after the end of
    40	   the window. The verifier makes no other comparison with the window [R5].
    41	
    42	T is a chain time. A chain time is the time that a Bitcoin block header states. It is not a measurement of real
    43	time [R6].
    44	
    45	## What a VALID receipt does not tell you
    46	
    47	A VALID verdict does not establish any of these [R7]:
    48	
    49	- who gave the bytes to the service;
    50	- what the bytes mean, or that they are true;
    51	- that a key was not revoked (the report says `revocation: NOT_EVALUATED`);
    52	- that a signature algorithm or key was still safe when it signed (`historical_security: NOT_EVALUATED`);
    53	- that the bytes existed after any time, or before other bytes;
    54	- any standing (the report says `standing: ABSENT/NOT_ISSUED_IN_PROFILE`);
    55	- that the two keys have different controllers. One controller holds both keys;
    56	- that the authority records were public.
    57	
    58	**Real time.** The verifier can also report a value U in a separate field, `utc_assessment`. U is a real-time
    59	upper bound only under an assumption, A-UTC, that the verifier does not check [R8]. Do not use U without that
    60	assumption. A-UTC says: at least one of six blocks states a chain time no more than two hours before its real
    61	production time [R9].
    62	
    63	**More than one receipt.** The same content can have more than one VALID receipt, each with its own T. Identify a
    64	receipt by the SHA-256 hash of its `A_sig` bytes, not by the hash of its files [R10]. `A_sig` is the bytes that
    65	hold both frames and their signatures.
    66	
    67	## Conditions that V1 does not check
    68	
    69	Each statement in "What a VALID V1 receipt tells you" is true only if these conditions are true [R2]:
    70	
    71	- Nobody misused the signing capability of the service.
    72	- SHA-256 and OpenTimestamps are not broken.
    73	- The Bitcoin chain has no reorganization of 6 or more blocks [R11].
    74	- Your verifier release is authentic.
    75	
    76	**Capture of the authority records.** An attacker can get control of both the DNS record and the git repository.
    77	Then the attacker can make new receipts under its own keys, and **the verifier says VALID for them** [R12]. V1 has
    78	no protection against this: the service-key listing was removed from V1 [R13]. The verifier cannot see this
    79	capture. V1 also does not check that the records were public [R14].
    80	
    81	Receipts that the honest service signed stay protected in that case. The attacker cannot move their signatures to
    82	other keys or other bytes [R12].
    83	
    84	**Capture of the service's AWS account.** An attacker with the AWS account can sign with both keys. Its receipts
    85	are VALID. V1 gives no protection against this [R15].
    86	
    87	## Before you check a receipt
    88	
    89	1. Get the verifier release from its publisher. Check the release signature with the published release key [R16].
    90	2. Do not use a release that is marked TEST for a real decision [R17].
    91	3. Get all files of the receipt from the holder. The holder is the person who keeps a receipt.
    92	4. Do not reformat the receipt files. A changed indentation makes the verdict INVALID [R18].
    93	
    94	## How to read the verdict
    95	
    96	The verifier gives one of three verdicts, with a reason code [R19]:
    97	
    98	| Verdict | Meaning | What you do |
    99	|---|---|---|
   100	| VALID (`VALID_STRICT`) | All V1 checks passed. | Rely only on the statements in "What a VALID V1 receipt tells you". |
   101	| INVALID | A check failed. | Reject the receipt. INVALID does not show that the content is false. |
   102	| CANNOT VERIFY (`UNVERIFIABLE`) | The verifier does not have what it needs. | Reject the receipt. Ask the holder for the missing files. |
   103	
   104	Three special rules apply [R20]:
   105	
   106	- The verifier can report a T with INVALID. That T is not evidence of a valid receipt.
   107	- `INVALID / ISSUANCE_WINDOW` can come from a later OpenTimestamps proof. Ask the holder for the service's
   108	  original proof.
   109	- The verifier cannot check that the git commit was public.
   110	
   111	## Decisions for each registered case
   112	
   113	The registered mapping lists these cases [R21]. For each case, the verifier uses the strict check only.
   114	
   115	| Case | Situation | Verdict | Your decision |
   116	|---|---|---|---|
   117	| a, g | An honest receipt, as the service shipped it. | VALID | Accept only this: these bytes were given to the service no later than chain time T. |
   118	| b | An attacker signs the content again with its own keys. | INVALID | Reject. |
   119	| c | Someone puts a receipt inside a second layer. | CANNOT VERIFY | Reject. |
   120	| d1 | Someone asks for a check with fewer checks than strict. | The verifier refuses. The strict check gives VALID. | Accept only the result of the strict check. |
   121	| d2 | An honest receipt without its git evidence. | CANNOT VERIFY | Reject. This is an honest receipt that V1 rejects. Ask the holder for the complete receipt. |
   122	| d3 | A copy with one attacker key and only one authority record. | INVALID | Reject. |
   123	| e, e′ | One key was revoked, before or after the issue time. | VALID | The receipt does not show that the key was not revoked. If your decision needs that, get other evidence. |
   124	| f | Another person sends content through the honest service. | VALID | The receipt does not show who sent the content. If your decision needs the sender, check the sender yourself. |
   125	| h | An honest receipt inside a renewal layer. | CANNOT VERIFY | Reject. This is an honest receipt that V1 rejects. |
   126	| i | The content is a contract with two valid signatures of the parties. | VALID | Accept only this: the signed contract existed by T. You must check the parties' signatures and keys yourself. |
   127	| i′ | The same, but one party signature does not verify. | VALID | Reject the contract. The receipt is valid, but your own check of a party signature fails. The service does not read the content. |
   128	
   129	Cases e, f and i′ have one hazard. The verdict is VALID, but the receipt does not answer your question [R22].
   130	
   131	## Open items in this draft
   132	
   133	- The release key and its fingerprint are not yet fixed. A TEST release key must never appear on this page [R23].
   134	- The scenario wording follows the registered mapping. A10 narrowed T to chain time and the window to two
   135	  comparisons; this page uses the narrowed meaning [R5][R6].
   136	- The registered text says the opening list is "verbatim" [R24]. The ruling asks for the list "nearly
   137	  verbatim" [R25]. This page copies it exactly.
   138	- A chain change to a DNSSEC algorithm outside {8, 13} makes new receipts CANNOT VERIFY. The service does not ship
   139	  those receipts [R26].
