   266	### A10.1.1 A conditional UTC assessment — AUTHOR ADOPTED 2026-10-18 (recommended by the owner and Codex), BROADEN
   267	
   268	Source: the decision memo, Part 1 (U1) and Part 3; its Codex review, F03, F04 and "Questions checked" 1; the
   269	tolerance evidence file. A10.1 stays as decided independently of this section.
   270	
   271	**Author ruling, 2026-10-18** (`docs/reviews/amendment-10-r4/AUTHOR-RULINGS.md`): *"That's it.  The other three
   272	items, while dense, seem rational are accepted."* And, confirming the owner's reading that this adopts A10.1.1,
   273	A10.4 option (e) with A10.4.1 and A10.4.2: *"Yes. That's my understanding.  I focused on reading those sections of
   274	the document.  A 10.4.2 stood out because you disagreed, but even for those where you were in agreement, I read what
   275	was proposed and accepted the recommendation as being "rationally based.""*
   276	
   277	**Notation.** h* is the receipt proof's selected inclusion height (A10.5). A2.1 counts it as confirmation one, so
   278	h*+5 is confirmation six. r(j) is the real (UTC) time at which block j was first produced.
   279	
   280	**Assumption A-UTC** (`assumption_id: "A-UTC-7200-6"`). *At least one of the six blocks h* … h*+5 has a header
   281	time no more than 7200 s before it was actually mined*, that is, `nTime(j) ≥ r(j) − 7200 s` for some j in
   282	[h*, h*+5]. A-UTC is an assumption of the UTC assessment only. It is not a Bitcoin consensus rule, the verifier
   283	does not check it, and no bundle carries evidence of it. It is a sufficient one-sided assumption, not a minimal one.
   284	Δ = 7200 s and m = 6 are frozen in the governing specification's catalog, not in the mutable trust file, and are
   285	not shown to be optimal.
   286	
   287	**Recommended claim text**, added after the A10.1 claim it qualifies (rows 20–21):
   288	
   289	> Under assumption A-UTC (at least one of the blocks h* … h*+5 has a header time no more than 7200 s before it was
   290	> actually mined; an assumption of the UTC assessment, not a consensus rule, and not checked by the verifier), the
   291	> bytes and both signatures existed before U = max nTime(h* … h*+5) + 7200 s, in UTC. V1 reports U only in
   292	> `utc_assessment`, beside its assumption identifier, outside the six stable fields. A2.1's refusal to bound the
   293	> observation lag B − C is unchanged.
   294	
   295	**Derivation** (memo, checked by Codex). (1) `A_sig` existed before r(h*), because h*'s merkle root commits to
   296	`SHA256(A_sig)` under the registered commitment and history assumptions. (2) r(h*) ≤ r(j) for every j ≥ h*,
   297	because block j commits to a chain that contains h*. (3) A-UTC supplies j in [h*, h*+5] with r(j) ≤ nTime(j) + 7200.
   298	(4) So r(h*) ≤ r(j) ≤ nTime(j) + 7200 ≤ U. The chain-order claims of A10.1 rest on steps 1–2 only.
   299	
   300	**Output contract** (memo, F03). `utc_assessment` contains `assumption_id: "A-UTC-7200-6"` and `utc_upper_bound`
   301	(RFC 3339 string or null). It is computed from the receipt proof's selected h* and its six qualified headers. Its
   302	bound is conditional on A-UTC and the existing commitment/history assumptions. It is null exactly when T is null.
   303	It does not change the six-field V1 stable result (A9:162, A9.4). Reporting a value with INVALID or UNVERIFIABLE
   304	does not establish a valid service receipt or valid service signatures. U is never a stable field, and V1's
   305	documentation and relying-party text never state U without A-UTC beside it.
   306	
   307	**Arithmetic and the 2106 boundary** (memo, F04). Verification of a preserved V1 receipt does not consult the
   308	current clock. Each bundled nTime is the profile's unsigned epoch-seconds value, widened before arithmetic:
   309	`U_seconds = 7200 + max(uint64(nTime(h*)), …, uint64(nTime(h*+5)))`, encoded as an RFC 3339 UTC string without
   310	reduction modulo 2^32 (for max nTime = 4294967295, U = `2106-02-07T08:28:15Z`, not a 1970 time). This 64-bit
   311	arithmetic covers preserved headers. Timestamp representation for Bitcoin blocks produced after the 2106 rollover
   312	of the 32-bit field is a Bitcoin protocol matter outside V1; the profile's serial-time unwrapping applies to DNSSEC
   313	RRSIG fields only.
   314	
   315	**What the ruling and signature do and do not do.** By his 2026-10-18 ruling and his signature, the author adopts
   316	A-UTC as a trust assumption. It does not establish that miners satisfy it, and nothing in V1 can.
   317	
   318	**Justification of the tolerance against the intended adversary**
   319	(`docs/decisions/2026-10-17-a-utc-tolerance-evidence.md`). The intended adversary is A9's backdater (A9:40): bytes
   320	that exist only at real time e, presented with a receipt whose U is earlier than e.
   321	- **Failure condition.** A-UTC fails, and U can be wrong, only if the miners of all six confirming blocks set
   322	  lagging timestamps: every block h* … h*+5 has a header time more than 7200 s before it was mined.
   323	- **A non-mining backdater** can choose only when to stamp and which proof to present. Lowest-height selection fixes
   324	  the result within a presented proof, but does not eliminate choice among available presentations. The UTC
   325	  assessment relies on A-UTC for the selected segment in every case. Over 559,718 retained complete six-block

